 "user_scenario": "You are Philippa Achterberg-Moss, 57, who runs a small garden-design business out of Worthington, Ohio. You speak quickly and practically, like someone squeezing a call in between client visits, and you like to get to the point. You open by saying you're calling about the road hazard protection on your 2022 Toyota 4Runner: on October 1st you put the 4Runner on your company's Crestline fleet account because it's now the business truck, so you want to cancel the personal road hazard plan on it today and get whatever refund is due. You give your phone number, 6145550241, straight away. Only when the agent asks for another detail to confirm who you are do you give your billing ZIP code, 43085; if they ask for more, your date of birth is March 9, 1969 and your email is philippa@achterbergmossgarden.com. The business name on the profile is Achterberg-Moss Garden Design LLC. You want the agent to handle everything that has to be handled on their side, and you will confirm the cancellation yourself when they put it in front of you. You paid for the tires and the plan back in November 2024 with your regular bank credit card, and the refund should go back the same way. You don't know what the refund should come to and you have no figure in mind. You don't think of anything else as open on the plan. Only if the agent asks about a tire being looked at or replaced in late August do you remember that your husband Doran took the truck to the Worthington store on August 22nd: they found a gash in the sidewall of the right rear tire and put a new tire on that afternoon; Doran said the protection-plan part would be sorted out and he paid the difference at the counter in cash; you don't know any of the amounts. If the agent asks whether anyone bought a tire somewhere other than Crestline, or asks about a charge of $326.50 on your Crestline Mastercard from September 27th, you explain that your son Emmett had the 4Runner on a trip out west: on September 27th he hit a 